## Why This Is Asked

Tests for optimistic UI and offline resilience are common in messaging apps. This question probes ability to design deterministic E2E tests that cover front-end and back-end coordination, error paths, and data isolation.

## Key Concepts

- Optimistic UI updates
- Network fault simulation and retry handling
- Test data isolation and deterministic seeding
- Front-end/back-end state reconciliation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright test snippet (simplified)
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('delete with optimistic update and Undo', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://chat.app');
  await page.fill('#newMessage', 'hello world');
  await page.click('#send');
  const msg = page.locator('.message').last();
  await expect(msg).toHaveText('hello world');

  await msg.click('.delete');
  await expect(page.locator('.toast-undo')).toBeVisible();
  await expect(page.locator('.message')).not.toContainText('hello world');

  await page.click('.toast-undo');
  await expect(page.locator('.message')).toContainText('hello world');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to handle multiple concurrent deletions in a single channel?
- How would you verify backend state during intermittent connectivity without flaky tests?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for per-user feature flags are common in SaaS apps. This question checks the ability to seed data, perform API-driven state changes, and verify UI behavior across login and page reloads in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- Per-user feature flags and API seed/reset
- End-to-end UI verification of dynamic layouts
- Test isolation and deterministic CI runs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example using Playwright-like syntax (pseudocode)
// seedUser(), setFlag(), login() are helper utilities
test('compact dashboard toggling', async ({ page, request }) => {
  const { userId, email, password } = await seedUser(request);
  await setFlag(request, userId, 'CompactDashboard', true);
  await login(page, email, password);
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-compact')).toBeVisible();
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-compact')).toBeVisible();
  await setFlag(request, userId, 'CompactDashboard', false);
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-full')).toBeVisible();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you guard against flaky UI assertions caused by caching?
- How would you extend the test to cover multiple user segments (roles/regions) and parallel execution?

